{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"919639d991ed4416","name":"card_event_repel_the_attack","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/ANZAC/events/card_event_repel_the_attack","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"REPEL THE ATTACK","text":"All enemy units Retreat. End the turn unless played from right-most in hand.","flavorText":"In August 1942 at Milne Bay, Australian forces faced a determined Japanese attempt to seize newly-built Allied airfields on the eastern tip of New Guinea. The Japanese badly underestimated the strength of the garrison, expecting only a few companies of defenders, while the Allies had reinforced the area with thousands of troops. The first line of defence was held largely by the Australian 7th Infantry Brigade, including the 9th, 25th and 61st Battalions. Despite being pushed back in several hard-fought engagements, these Militia troops slowed the Japanese advance and helped hold the approaches to the airfields. Veteran troops of the 18th Infantry Brigade, part of the 7th Division, were then committed to the fighting and launched counterattacks that steadily forced the Japanese eastward. RAAF Kittyhawks from Nos. 75 and 76 Squadrons also played a crucial role, attacking Japanese troops, landing craft and supply lines. By 7 September, the Japanese had withdrawn. The victory at Milne Bay became the first full-scale defeat of Japanese land forces in the Pacific theater during WWII and provided an important boost to Allied morale.","faction":"Anzac","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_repel_the_attack","title":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_repel_the_attack_title","SourceString":"REPEL THE ATTACK","LocalizedString":"REPEL THE ATTACK"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Anzac","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_repel_the_attack_text","SourceString":"All enemy units Retreat. End the turn unless played from right-most in hand.","LocalizedString":"All enemy units Retreat. End the turn unless played from right-most in hand."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_repel_the_attack_history","SourceString":"In August 1942 at Milne Bay, Australian forces faced a determined Japanese attempt to seize newly-built Allied airfields on the eastern tip of New Guinea. The Japanese badly underestimated the strength of the garrison, expecting only a few companies of defenders, while the Allies had reinforced the area with thousands of troops. The first line of defence was held largely by the Australian 7th Infantry Brigade, including the 9th, 25th and 61st Battalions. Despite being pushed back in several hard-fought engagements, these Militia troops slowed the Japanese advance and helped hold the approaches to the airfields. Veteran troops of the 18th Infantry Brigade, part of the 7th Division, were then committed to the fighting and launched counterattacks that steadily forced the Japanese eastward. RAAF Kittyhawks from Nos. 75 and 76 Squadrons also played a crucial role, attacking Japanese troops, landing craft and supply lines. By 7 September, the Japanese had withdrawn. The victory at Milne Bay became the first full-scale defeat of Japanese land forces in the Pacific theater during WWII and provided an important boost to Allied morale.","LocalizedString":"In August 1942 at Milne Bay, Australian forces faced a determined Japanese attempt to seize newly-built Allied airfields on the eastern tip of New Guinea. The Japanese badly underestimated the strength of the garrison, expecting only a few companies of defenders, while the Allies had reinforced the area with thousands of troops. The first line of defence was held largely by the Australian 7th Infantry Brigade, including the 9th, 25th and 61st Battalions. Despite being pushed back in several hard-fought engagements, these Militia troops slowed the Japanese advance and helped hold the approaches to the airfields. Veteran troops of the 18th Infantry Brigade, part of the 7th Division, were then committed to the fighting and launched counterattacks that steadily forced the Japanese eastward. RAAF Kittyhawks from Nos. 75 and 76 Squadrons also played a crucial role, attacking Japanese troops, landing craft and supply lines. By 7 September, the Japanese had withdrawn. The victory at Milne Bay became the first full-scale defeat of Japanese land forces in the Pacific theater during WWII and provided an important boost to Allied morale."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_3.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/REPEL_THE_ATTACK.REPEL_THE_ATTACK","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_repel_the_attack_photo","SourceString":"An Australian gun crew during the Battle of Milne Bay, New Guinea, 1942.","LocalizedString":"An Australian gun crew during the Battle of Milne Bay, New Guinea, 1942."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[{"name":"cardsToRetreat","type":"TArray<UBaseCardObject*>","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_repel_the_attack","owner":"card_event_repel_the_attack_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"Temp_int_Array_Index_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_Add_IntInt_ReturnValue","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"K2Node_Event_targetCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{